-- title:
--   Functions supported on rank-one frequencies
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Consider functions on Boolean matrices $\{0,1\}^{\iota\times\kappa}$.
--
--   1. **Rank-one support.** $f$ is supported on rank-one frequencies if
--   $$
--   \hat f(P)\ne0\implies\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}(P)\le1 ,
--   $$
--   i.e. its Fourier expansion only involves the empty frequency and rectangles $s\times t$. For such $f$, damped expectations are polynomials in the damping parameters determined by the rectangular sums.
